# Primary Assessment, part 2: general impression, circulation, chief complaint and life threats,
# baseline vital signs and the need for rapid treatment or transport (NREMT EMR test plan, 2025:
# Primary Assessment 37% to 41%, split by Tigertest). EMR scope (National EMS Scope of Practice
# Model 2019): manual blood pressure (automated blood pressure, pulse oximetry and glucose
# monitoring are EMT and above); direct pressure, tourniquet and wound packing. Hemorrhage and
# shock figures: NASEMSO National Model EMS Clinical Guidelines v3.0, Extremity Trauma/External
# Hemorrhage Management and Shock.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 1, "General impression", "As you walk up, a man sits slumped, pale and silent with blood soaking his pant leg. What does your general impression tell you?",
      ["He looks sick and needs fast action", "He looks stable for now", "He needs a full history before any other care", "He should walk to the ambulance with help"],
      "The general impression is your first look from across the room. Pallor, silence and heavy bleeding say the patient is sick, so you move straight to the life threats."),
    S(1, 2, "Life-threatening bleeding", "An awake man has blood spurting from a deep cut on his upper arm. What comes first?",
      ["Control the bleeding", "Count his breathing rate", "Take his blood pressure", "Ask about his allergies"],
      "Massive bleeding can kill in minutes, so it is controlled as soon as you see it, even before a full airway and breathing check in an awake, talking patient."),
    S(1, 2, "Pulse site", "Where do you check the pulse of an unresponsive adult?",
      ["Carotid artery in the neck", "Radial artery at the wrist", "Brachial artery on the inside of the upper arm", "Pedal pulse on the foot"],
      "In an unresponsive adult, check the carotid pulse, which is felt even when blood pressure is low. In an infant, use the brachial pulse inside the upper arm."),
    S(1, 2, "Skin signs", "Which skin finding best suggests poor perfusion?",
      ["Pale, cool and moist skin", "Pink, warm and dry skin", "Red, flushed skin after hard exercise", "Tanned skin with freckles"],
      "When blood flow drops, the body pulls blood from the skin, leaving it pale, cool and clammy. Pink, warm, dry skin is normal."),
    S(1, 2, "Tourniquet", "Direct pressure does not stop heavy bleeding from a forearm wound. Where do you place a tourniquet?",
      ["2 to 3 inches above the wound", "Directly over the wound", "Over the elbow joint", "Below the wound near the hand"],
      "Place the tourniquet on bare skin 2 to 3 inches above the wound, not over a joint, and tighten it until the bleeding stops. Write down the time it went on."),
    S(1, 3, "Tourniquet pain", "A patient with a properly placed tourniquet complains that it hurts badly. What should you do?",
      ["Leave it tight and explain why", "Loosen it a little every few minutes", "Take it off and use a pressure bandage", "Move it below the wound so the hand gets blood"],
      "A working tourniquet is painful. Do not loosen it to relieve pain: bleeding will restart. Reassure the patient and let the next level of care know when it was applied."),
    S(1, 2, "Shock", "A woman hit by a car is anxious, pale and has a fast, weak pulse. Her blood pressure is still normal. What do you suspect?",
      ["Early shock", "Simple anxiety", "A heart attack", "No problem while the pressure is normal"],
      "Restlessness, pallor and a fast pulse are early signs of shock. Blood pressure often stays normal until late, especially in young people, so do not wait for it to fall."),
    S(1, 2, "Blood pressure", "Which blood pressure method is within EMR scope under the national model?",
      ["Manual cuff and stethoscope", "Automated cuff only", "Arterial line reading from a monitor", "Pulse oximeter estimate from the finger"],
      "The national scope model lists manual blood pressure at the EMR level. Automated blood pressure devices are listed at the EMT level and above."),
    M(1, 2, "Rapid transport", "Which findings in the primary assessment mean the patient needs rapid transport and more help? Select two.",
      ["Responds only to pain", "Bleeding that is hard to control", "A sprained wrist", "A small scraped knee", "Normal breathing and speech"],
      "An altered patient and uncontrolled bleeding are priority findings. They call for early transport by EMS and advanced help, while minor injuries can wait."),
    X(1, 2, "Pulse sites", "For each patient, choose the best place to check the pulse first.",
      ["Unresponsive adult", "Responsive adult", "Unresponsive infant", "Responsive 7-year-old"],
      ["Carotid", "Radial", "Brachial"], [0, 1, 2, 1],
      "Check the carotid in an unresponsive adult and the brachial in an infant. In a responsive adult or older child, the radial pulse at the wrist is easiest and also shows the pressure is enough to reach the wrist."),
    S(1, 1, "Chief complaint", "What is the chief complaint?",
      ["The problem in the patient's own words", "The diagnosis you suspect after your exam", "The worst injury you see", "The reason dispatch gave"],
      "The chief complaint is why the patient called, ideally in their own words, such as I can't breathe. Record it as said, not as your guess at a diagnosis."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 2, "Capillary refill", "In which patient is capillary refill most useful as a sign of perfusion?",
      ["A 3-year-old with vomiting", "A 70-year-old in a cold room", "An adult with cold hands outdoors", "A patient wearing nail polish"],
      "Capillary refill is most reliable in young children, such as a 3-year-old. Cold temperatures, age and some medicines make it unreliable in adults."),
    S(2, 2, "Wound packing", "Heavy bleeding comes from a deep wound in the groin where a tourniquet will not fit. What should you do?",
      ["Pack the wound and hold firm pressure", "Lay a thin bandage on top and watch closely", "Raise the leg and wait", "Apply ice and watch it"],
      "Junctional wounds such as the groin or armpit cannot take a limb tourniquet. Packing gauze deep into the wound and holding hard pressure stops the bleeding. Wound packing is within EMR scope."),
    S(2, 2, "Shock positioning", "How should you position a patient in shock with no suspected spine or leg injury?",
      ["Flat on the back and kept warm", "Sitting upright in a chair", "Head down on a steep slope with legs up", "Standing to help blood reach the legs"],
      "Lay the patient flat and prevent heat loss with blankets. Steep head-down positions are no longer advised, and sitting up can worsen low blood flow to the brain."),
    S(2, 2, "Pulse check", "You find a pulse of 44 in an adult who is pale and confused. What does this mean?",
      ["A slow rate with poor perfusion is serious", "A normal resting rate", "A sign of good fitness in a healthy adult", "Nothing until you check it again in 15 minutes"],
      "A rate under 60 with signs of poor perfusion such as confusion and pallor is dangerous and needs rapid transport and advanced help. A slow pulse is only reassuring in a well, alert, fit person."),
    S(2, 3, "Internal bleeding", "A man kicked by a horse has a bruised belly, thirst and a fast, weak pulse. What is the best care?",
      ["Treat for shock and speed his transport", "Have him drink water for his thirst", "Massage the bruised area to ease the pain", "Wait to see if the bruise grows before acting"],
      "These signs point to internal bleeding you cannot stop in the field. Keep him flat and warm, give oxygen, and get him to a hospital quickly. Nothing by mouth."),
    M(2, 2, "Tourniquet use", "Which steps are correct when applying a tourniquet? Select three.",
      ["Place it on bare skin", "Tighten until bleeding stops", "Note the time it was applied", "Cover it with clothing", "Loosen it every 10 minutes", "Place it over the knee"],
      "A tourniquet goes on bare skin above the wound, is tightened until the bleeding stops and has its time recorded where everyone can see it. It must stay visible and must not be loosened."),
    O(2, 2, "Primary assessment", "Put the parts of the primary assessment of a responsive trauma patient in order.",
      ["Form a general impression", "Check responsiveness", "Control major bleeding", "Check the airway and breathing", "Decide on transport priority"],
      "After the first look and a check of responsiveness, life-threatening bleeding is handled at once, then airway and breathing, and the findings set how urgent the patient is."),
    S(2, 1, "Vital signs", "Why take a set of baseline vital signs early?",
      ["To compare with later sets", "To make a diagnosis", "To replace the history", "To decide whether to wear gloves or not"],
      "Baseline vitals give you a starting point. Repeated sets show whether the patient is getting better or worse, which matters more than any single number."),
    X(2, 2, "Priority", "For each patient, choose whether care is a high priority or can wait.",
      ["Fast, weak pulse with pale skin", "Unresponsive after a fall", "Pain in one finger", "Trouble breathing with a pale face", "Calm and alert with a small bruise"],
      ["High priority", "Can wait"], [0, 0, 1, 0, 1],
      "Signs of shock, an unresponsive patient and breathing trouble are priority findings. A painful finger or a small bruise in an alert patient can wait."),
    S(2, 2, "Nosebleed", "A conscious patient has a heavy nosebleed. How should she sit?",
      ["Leaning forward while pinching the nose", "Leaning back with the head tilted toward the ceiling", "Lying flat on her back with her head turned", "Lying on her stomach"],
      "Leaning forward keeps blood from running down the throat, where it can be swallowed or inhaled, and firm pinching of the soft part of the nose slows the bleeding."),
    S(2, 2, "Impaled object", "A worker has a metal rod stuck in his thigh with little bleeding. What should you do?",
      ["Stabilize it in place with bulky dressings", "Pull it out and press on the wound", "Twist it to check how deep it goes", "Cut it flush with the skin yourself"],
      "Leave an impaled object in place, since it may be plugging a bleeding vessel. Pad around it to keep it from moving and control bleeding around it. Removal happens at the hospital, except an object blocking the airway or CPR."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 2, "Pulse rate", "Which resting pulse is within the normal range for an adult?",
      ["72 a minute", "40 a minute", "130 a minute", "150 a minute"],
      "A normal adult resting pulse is about 60 to 100, so 72 is normal. Children and infants have faster normal rates."),
    S(0, 2, "Pediatric shock", "Which sign of shock in a child tends to appear last?",
      ["A falling blood pressure", "A fast heart rate that keeps climbing", "Cool hands and feet with mottled skin", "Slow capillary refill in the fingertips"],
      "Children hold their blood pressure up until they are near collapse. A fast pulse, cool skin and slow refill come first; low blood pressure is a late and ominous sign."),
    S(0, 2, "Direct pressure", "Blood soaks through the gauze you are holding on a leg wound. What should you do?",
      ["Add more gauze and press harder", "Lift the gauze to look", "Remove the gauze and start over", "Rinse the wound with water"],
      "Lifting or removing soaked gauze pulls away clots. Add gauze on top and press harder; if bleeding still will not stop on a limb, apply a tourniquet."),
    S(0, 2, "Manual blood pressure", "When taking a manual blood pressure, what is the systolic reading?",
      ["The first tapping sound you hear", "The point the sounds stop", "The highest number you pumped to", "The average of the two numbers"],
      "As the cuff slowly deflates, the first clear tapping sound marks the systolic pressure. The point where the sounds disappear is the diastolic pressure."),
    S(0, 2, "Palpated pressure", "In a noisy place you cannot hear the pulse sounds. How can you estimate systolic pressure?",
      ["By feeling for the radial pulse to return", "By guessing from skin color", "By counting breaths", "By pressing on a fingernail and counting the refill"],
      "Inflate the cuff until the radial pulse disappears, then let it down slowly. The reading when the pulse returns is the palpated systolic pressure. Record it as palpated."),
    S(0, 2, "Pulse quality", "What does a thready pulse mean?",
      ["It is weak and hard to feel", "It is strong and bounding", "It skips every third beat in a regular pattern", "It is slower than normal for the age"],
      "A thready pulse is weak and faint, often fast, and is a common sign of shock. A bounding pulse is unusually strong."),
    M(0, 2, "Signs of shock", "Which signs fit a patient in shock? Select three.",
      ["Restlessness or anxiety", "Fast, weak pulse", "Pale, sweaty skin", "Warm, pink skin", "Slow, deep breathing", "Strong, slow pulse"],
      "Shock makes the brain uneasy, speeds the pulse while weakening it, and pulls blood from the skin. Warm pink skin and a strong slow pulse are not typical of early shock."),
    S(0, 2, "Pediatric assessment", "How can you form a quick first impression of a young child before you touch her?",
      ["Watch her look, breathing and color", "Take a blood pressure with a small cuff", "Ask the parent for her full medical history", "Check her temperature under the arm"],
      "Watching from the doorway shows how the child looks and acts, how hard she works to breathe and the color of her skin. A child who is upset by touch may look worse once handled."),
    S(0, 2, "Improvised tourniquet", "Why is a commercial tourniquet preferred over a belt?",
      ["It reliably tightens enough to stop flow", "It is cheaper to replace", "It does not need to be timed or marked", "It can be loosened every few minutes to rest the limb"],
      "Improvised tourniquets often fail to get tight enough and can just slow venous return, which makes bleeding worse. Use a commercial device when one is available."),
    S(0, 2, "Vital signs", "A trauma patient's pulse rises from 92 to 118 over 10 minutes while his skin grows paler. What does this trend suggest?",
      ["He is getting worse", "He is getting better", "He is calming down", "The first reading was wrong"],
      "A rising pulse with paler skin suggests worsening shock. Trends across repeated vitals tell you more than one set and should be reported to the next level of care."),
]
